{"id": 3650689, "title": "CVE-2026-5773: wrong reuse of SMB connection", "submitted": "2026-04-05", "source_url": "https://hackerone.com/reports/3650689", "excerpt": "### Affected Version & File\n**Version:** curl 8.19.0 (and master up to `2e5d219205ebec64a66bfd29bba73dd5049c434f`), and likely older versions supporting SMB.\n**Files:** `lib/url.c` (at `url_match_proto_config`) and `lib/smb.c`\n\n### What we found\n`libcurl` incorrectly reuses SMB connections across different shares on the same server, leading to data spoofing and access control bypass. \n\nWhen `libcurl` connects to an SMB share, the `smb_setup_connection` function parses the URL and saves the target share name into the connection's metadata (`smbc->share`).\n\nHowever, during connection reuse, `url_match_conn` only verifies the hostname, port, and credentials. It does not verify if the new URL's SMB share matches the existing connection's `smbc->share`. There is no `smb_conns_match` equivalent in `url_match_proto_config`.\n\n```c\n// lib/url.c\nstatic bool url_match_proto_config(struct connectdata *conn,\n                                   struct url_conn_match *m)\n{\n  if(!url_match_http_version(conn, m))\n    return FALSE;\n\n#ifdef USE_SSH\n  if(get_protocol_family(m->needle->scheme) & PROTO_FAMILY_SSH) {\n    if(!ssh_config_matches(m->needle, conn))\n      return FALSE;\n  }\n#endif\n#ifndef CURL_DISABLE_FTP\n  else if(get_protocol_family(m->needle->scheme) & PROTO_FAMILY_FTP) {\n    if(!ftp_conns_match(m->needle, conn))\n      return FALSE;\n  }\n#endif\n  /* VULNERABILITY: Missing SMB share matching here! */\n  return TRUE;\n}\n```\n\nBecause of this, when the connection is reused for a different share, `libcurl` calls `smb_do`, which advances the multi state machine to the `DOING` phase since `smbc->share` is already set from the first request. Then, `smb_request_state` is invoked. It retrieves the old `smbc` (which retains the share name from the *first* request) and the new `req` (which contains the `req->path` from the *new* request). \n\n`libcurl` then sends a `TREE_CONNECT` for the old share, followed by an `NT_CREATE_ANDX` for the new path. As a result, `libcurl` silently fetches `old_share/new_path` instead of `new_share/new_path`.\n\n### What we proved\nYou can replicate this by setting up a Samba server (default configuration) and fetching different files sequentially via curl. The following PoC commands demonstrate the issue on a Linux system against the latest curl `8.19.0` build:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Setup Samba server locally for the PoC\nsudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install", "excerpt_truncated": true, "final": "likely_valid", "final_label": "Confirmed by the code", "tone": "good", "explain": "The real code shows the flaw the report describes.", "confidence": 0.95, "judge_verdict": "likely_valid", "receipts_override": "", "policy_section": "", "grounding": {"verdict": "grounded", "rev": "acda4eae5eeb", "rev_source": "master on 2026-04-05", "counts": {"grounded": 19, "ungrounded_strong": 0, "ungrounded_medium": 0}, "problems": []}, "summary": "libcurl incorrectly reuses SMB connections across different shares on the same server, leading to data spoofing and access control bypass.", "claims": [{"id": "C1", "kind": "flaw", "text": "libcurl incorrectly reuses SMB connections across different shares on the same server, leading to data spoofing and access control bypass.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The code confirms that url_match_proto_config performs protocol-specific connection matching for SSH and FTP but has no equivalent check for SMB. This allows connections to be reused across different SMB shares on the same server.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 977, "note": "url_match_proto_config lacks SMB share matching while having protocol-specific matching for SSH (lines 984-987) and FTP (lines 990-993)", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/ftp.c", "line": 4462, "note": "ftp_conns_match exists and compares FTP-specific connection metadata", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 700, "note": "ssh_config_matches exists and compares SSH-specific connection metadata", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C2", "kind": "location", "text": "The vulnerability is located in `lib/url.c` in the `url_match_proto_config` function, which does not verify if the new URL's SMB share matches the existing connection's `smbc->share`.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The vulnerability location is correctly identified. url_match_proto_config is called during connection reuse evaluation and lacks SMB share verification.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 977, "note": "url_match_proto_config function definition shows no SMB share matching logic", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 1243, "note": "url_match_conn calls url_match_proto_config at line 1243 as part of connection reuse decision", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C3", "kind": "trigger", "text": "When a connection is reused for a different share, `libcurl` calls `smb_do`, advancing the multi state machine to the `DOING` phase since `smbc->share` is already set from the first request.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "When a connection is reused, smbc retains the share from the first request. smb_do returns early because smbc->share is set, and smb_request_state proceeds with the old smbc->share but new req->path.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1195, "note": "smb_do function checks if smbc->share is already set at line 1204 and returns CURLE_OK early", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1003, "note": "smb_request_state retrieves smbc from connection metadata at line 1003", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1004, "note": "smb_request_state retrieves req (with new request's path) from easy handle metadata at line 1004", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C4", "kind": "impact", "text": "The application silently fetches `old_share/new_path` instead of `new_share/new_path`, causing data spoofing.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The code path shows TREE_CONNECT is sent for the old share (smbc->share) while the file operation targets the new path (req->path), resulting in old_share/new_path access.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1023, "note": "smb_request_state calls smb_send_tree_connect with smbc (containing old share) at line 1023", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 1065, "note": "Subsequent NT_CREATE_ANDX processing uses req->fid from the response, operating on the new request's path within the old share", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C5", "kind": "reproduction", "text": "The provided PoC script demonstrates that after fetching a file from `share1`, a subsequent request to `share2` returns the content from `share1` instead of `share2`.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The PoC is logically consistent with the code flaw. While I cannot execute it, the described behavior matches the code analysis: first request sets smbc->share, second request reuses connection and incorrectly accesses first share's content.", "citations": [{"file": "poc.sh", "line": 0, "note": "PoC script demonstrates the issue by making sequential requests to different shares on the same server", "valid": false}]}], "external_checks": [{"statement": "The report recommends implementing an smb_conns_match function within lib/smb.c and calling it from url_match_proto_config logic (similar to FTP and SSH) in lib/url.c", "status": "not_addressed", "url": "https://developer.apple.com/documentation/driverkit/iouserclientmethoddispatch/function", "quote": ""}, {"statement": "The report recommends implementing an smb_conns_match function within lib/smb.c and calling it from url_match_proto_config logic (similar to FTP and SSH) in lib/url.c", "status": "not_addressed", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/questions/5857397/what-are-the-examples-of-match-function", "quote": ""}, {"statement": "The report recommends implementing an smb_conns_match function within lib/smb.c and calling it from url_match_proto_config logic (similar to FTP and SSH) in lib/url.c", "status": "not_addressed", "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-my/answers/questions/5857397/what-are-the-examples-of-match-function", "quote": ""}], "external_sources": [{"url": "https://developer.apple.com/documentation/driverkit/iouserclientmethoddispatch/function", "title": "function | Apple Developer Documentation", "for": "The report recommends implementing an `smb_conns_match` function within `lib/smb.c` and calling it from the `url_match_proto_config` logic (similar to FTP and SSH) in `lib/url.c` that explicitly verif"}, {"url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/questions/5185397/what-are-the-examples-of-match-function", "title": "what are the examples of match function - Microsoft Q&A", "for": "The report recommends implementing an `smb_conns_match` function within `lib/smb.c` and calling it from the `url_match_proto_config` logic (similar to FTP and SSH) in `lib/url.c` that explicitly verif"}, {"url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-my/answers/questions/5185397/what-are-the-examples-of-match-function", "title": "what are the examples of match function - Microsoft Q&A", "for": "The report recommends implementing an `smb_conns_match` function within `lib/smb.c` and calling it from the `url_match_proto_config` logic (similar to FTP and SSH) in `lib/url.c` that explicitly verif"}], "key_reasons": ["url_match_proto_config has protocol-specific matching for SSH and FTP but completely lacks SMB share matching", "Connection reuse logic in url_match_conn calls url_match_proto_config, allowing cross-share reuse", "smb_do returns early when smbc->share is set, causing smb_request_state to use stale share with new path", "This enables data spoofing across trust boundaries (shares) on the same server", "The attack scenario is realistic: an application accessing multiple shares with same credentials can be tricked into reading attacker-controlled data from one share when requesting from another"], "missing_info": [], "reply": "Thank you for the detailed report and PoC. The code confirms the flaw: url_match_proto_config (lib/url.c:977-996) performs protocol-specific matching for SSH and FTP but has no SMB share check. This allows connection reuse across different shares. When reused, smb_do (lib/smb.c:1204) returns early because smbc->share is set from the first request, and smb_request_state then sends TREE_CONNECT for the old share while operating on the new path. This is a valid security issue enabling cross-share data spoofing. We will implement an smb_conns_match function and add the check to url_match_proto_config.", "code": [{"name": "lib/ftp.c", "start": 4462, "end": 4475, "reason": "definition of ftp_conns_match", "rows": [{"n": 4462, "text": "bool ftp_conns_match(struct connectdata *needle, struct connectdata *conn)", "notes": ["C1: ftp_conns_match exists and compares FTP-specific connection metadata"]}, {"n": 4463, "text": "{", "notes": []}, {"n": 4464, "text": "  struct ftp_conn *nftpc = Curl_conn_meta_get(needle, CURL_META_FTP_CONN);", "notes": []}, {"n": 4465, "text": "  struct ftp_conn *cftpc = Curl_conn_meta_get(conn, CURL_META_FTP_CONN);", "notes": []}, {"n": 4466, "text": "  /* Also match ACCOUNT, ALTERNATIVE-TO-USER, USE_SSL and CCC options */", "notes": []}, {"n": 4467, "text": "  if(!nftpc || !cftpc ||", "notes": []}, {"n": 4468, "text": "     Curl_timestrcmp(nftpc->account, cftpc->account) ||", "notes": []}, {"n": 4469, "text": "     Curl_timestrcmp(nftpc->alternative_to_user,", "notes": []}, {"n": 4470, "text": "                     cftpc->alternative_to_user) ||", "notes": []}, {"n": 4471, "text": "     (nftpc->use_ssl != cftpc->use_ssl) ||", "notes": []}, {"n": 4472, "text": "     (nftpc->ccc != cftpc->ccc))", "notes": []}, {"n": 4473, "text": "    return FALSE;", "notes": []}, {"n": 4474, "text": "  return TRUE;", 